@TITLE@ QilinRecomp for macOS (Apple silicon and Intel)
==============================================================

@FULL_TITLE@ (@STUDIO@), recompiled to run natively: the game's own
program translated into C++ and compiled for your Mac, drawn with Vulkan
(through MoltenVK, which comes with the app). It is a work in progress: it
reaches the races on Windows, but some drawing is still wrong, and it has
not yet been played on a Mac.

No game data is included, and no PS3 keys. You need your own copy of the
game: @RELEASE@. Other regions and versions are other programs and aren't
supported.


Your game folder
----------------
You need three things, all from your own disc and console software:

1. The disc's files: the folder holding PS3_GAME (with PS3_DISC.SFB beside
   it), copied from your disc as it is.
2. The decrypted program. The disc's PS3_GAME/USRDIR/EBOOT.BIN is
   encrypted, and this project can't decrypt it. Do it once with the PS3
   emulator RPCS3 (https://rpcs3.net): in RPCS3 choose Utilities > Decrypt
   PS3 Binaries and pick that EBOOT.BIN. RPCS3 writes EBOOT.elf beside it;
   if it ends up anywhere else, move it into PS3_GAME/USRDIR, beside
   EBOOT.BIN.
3. The PS3's fonts, from its system software. In RPCS3 choose File >
   Install Firmware and pick the PS3 system software update (PS3UPDAT.PUP,
   from PlayStation's website). RPCS3 keeps it in your home folder's
   Library/Application Support/rpcs3/dev_flash, where the game finds it by
   itself. A dev_flash folder copied into your game folder, beside
   PS3_GAME, is found too.

The app checks all three when you choose the folder, and says what is
missing and what to do.


Playing
-------
1. Drag @TITLE@.app onto Applications (in this disk image's window).
   Building the game needs about 2 GB free.
2. Open @TITLE@ from Applications. The first time, macOS stops it, because
   the app isn't signed by an Apple developer account (see below):
   - open System Settings, then Privacy & Security;
   - scroll down to Security, where it says "@TITLE@ was blocked to
     protect your Mac" (or "... was blocked from use because it is not
     from an identified developer");
   - click Open Anyway, then Open Anyway again in the window that appears,
     and confirm with your password or Touch ID.
   macOS asks only once. (On macOS 14, right-clicking the app in Finder and
   choosing Open does the same.)
3. The game is compiled with Apple's own compiler, free from Apple (its
   licence doesn't let it come with this download). If your Mac doesn't
   have it yet, the app says so: click "Install Apple's command line
   tools", follow Apple's installer (a few minutes), then click "Check
   again".
4. Click "Choose your game folder" and pick the folder above, or drag it
   onto the window.
5. Wait for the first build: a few minutes. The game's code is recompiled
   from your EBOOT.elf and compiled, as many files at once as your Mac's
   memory allows (each takes up to about @COMPILE_MEMORY_MB@ MB). Your game
   files never leave your Mac.
6. Click Play. The window hides while the game runs.

After that, the app remembers your folder, and Play starts the game
straight away. After an update it builds the game again first, recompiling
only what changed. Leave the game folder where it is: the game reads its
files from it while you play.

The game's output is in run.log and run-errors.log, and the build's in
build.log, all in ~/Library/Application Support/qilin/@GAME@/work (in
Finder: Go, Go to Folder). Send them when reporting a problem.

About "Open Anyway": the app is signed only by itself ("ad hoc"), not by
an Apple developer account, so Gatekeeper can't name who made it. Check
that you downloaded it from the project's GitHub releases page (below)
before you open it. Nothing in it changes that check or works around it.

Needs: macOS 14.5 (Sonoma) or newer, on an Apple silicon or Intel Mac,
8 GB of memory or more, and Apple's command line tools from Xcode 16 or
newer, which need that macOS (the app checks; older tools are updated with
System Settings, General, Software Update). Graphics go through MoltenVK,
Vulkan over Apple's Metal.


Controls
--------
A controller works as the PS3's. On the keyboard:

  Keys            Button          Keys              Button
  arrows          d-pad           Q, W              L1, R1
  Z               cross           1, 2              L2, R2
  X               circle          Return            Start
  A               square          Delete            Select
  S               triangle        C, V              L3, R3
  I J K L         left stick      keypad 8 4 2 6    right stick

F1 or Esc (a controller: Back and Start together): the settings menu (the
game pauses), with the controls (set by pressing them), the picture and
the console's own settings. Option+Return, F11 or the window's green
button: full screen. F3: frame times. On a Mac keyboard F1, F3 and F11 may
need the fn key.

Settings (ridgeracer7.ini), saves (dev_hdd0) and the shader cache are kept
in ~/Library/Application Support/qilin/@GAME@, with the folder you chose,
the build (work) and its cache of compiled files (build-cache).


What's in the app
-----------------
Right-click @TITLE@.app, Show Package Contents:

Contents/MacOS/Play @TITLE@     chooses your game folder, builds the
                                  game (once) and starts it
Contents/Resources/kit/qilin      the recompiler: turns your @BOOT@ into
                                  C++, and the game's shaders into Vulkan's
Contents/Resources/kit/lib, include
                                  the prebuilt rest of the game: its
                                  runtime, the renderer, sound, films, the
                                  window and controls (SDL3), the menu
                                  (Dear ImGui), fonts (FreeType), MPEG-2
                                  and ATRAC3plus (FFmpeg's libavcodec)
Contents/Resources/kit/files.txt  every file this release holds (see below)
Contents/Frameworks/libMoltenVK.dylib
                                  MoltenVK: Vulkan over Metal (Khronos)
Contents/Resources/LICENSES       the licences of everything included

Portions of this software are copyright (C) 2024 The FreeType Project
(https://freetype.org). All rights reserved. FFmpeg's libraries are under
the LGPL: Contents/Resources/LICENSES/FFmpeg says where their source is
and how the game can be linked with other builds of them.

The game itself (@EXE@), built from your copy, is in the work folder
above, not in the app: the app is never changed by building.

Source, changes and issues: https://github.com/@REPO@


Updates and recovery
--------------------
"Check for updates" in the app asks GitHub for a newer release (it sends
nothing about you or your Mac). If there is one, it downloads the disk
image, copies the new app beside this one and swaps it in, then starts
again and builds the game for the new version. If anything fails on the
way, the old version is left as it was. The app must be somewhere you can
write to for that (Applications is, for an administrator): run from the
disk image itself, it can't update. You can also drag a new download's app
over this one in Applications: it finds your folder and the cache by
itself. Your saves and settings are never in the app, so nothing of yours
is touched.

The game is compiled for this Mac's processor; on another Mac, it is built
again. Compiled files come from the cache when nothing they're made from has
changed. A failed build leaves the working game as it was; the one before a
new build is kept as work/@EXE@.previous until the new one has run and
closed normally. "Build again from scratch" compiles everything anew.
